{"id":"u-5273199e-0125-4e00-882e-7db1ac219df6","src":"community","title":"Intermediate Plateau Buster 4 day FB","author":"John M.","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/W2xIZ2-intermediate-plateau-buster","desc":"I designed this because I was frustrated and stuck. I want to help you get through the intermediate phase. Intermediate Plateau Buster 4 Day FB** is designed specifically for those looking to break through training plateaus. Specific focus on bench press, over 12 weeks, you'll engage in a structured routine that incorporates a mix of compound lifts and targeted accessories, ensuring comprehensive muscle engagement and growth. \n\nAnyone who says they have the exact program for you is full of it, people respond differently to different volumes, frequencies etc., but if you have tried other intermediate programs and stalled give this program a shot. \n\nI have my suggestions for supplementary and accessory lifts but add your own so long as they hit they muscle groups and stick to the prescribed rep ranges and intensity. I do arms and lower body machine work on days I have extra time. For the first month or so I recommend keeping arms and things like leg extensions and leg curls to weekly at most. For core I suggest ab wheel, for spone stability -> suitcase and farmers carries once a week each on separate days. Once reached 2 min on carries you should increase the weight. \n\nEach main lift will have a day dedicated to working on form and technique while the accessory exercises will give you low stress volume to improve your main lift %s. \n\n3 blocks, each 4 weeks, will help you to reset monthly and week 1s will function as enough of a deload to set you up for the rest of the month. Reps drop each month as the weight increases. Aim to add weight weekly to main lift top set weekly. I put % targets but these can be altered slightly for your preference. If using rpe start each block around rpe 5-6 then move up ~ 1 rpe weekly so week 2 rpe 7, 8 and each block ends around rpe 8-9 then restarts next block (4 weeks) back at around rpe 5 or 6 depending on goals and fatigue. ONLY increase back-off weight if top set progress is slowing down or stalled and only increase ~5% at a time ie 60-->65%. \n\nFor accessory lifts (8-12 rep range) use double progression, when you hit 12+ reps on both sets add weight. These lifts drive hypertrophy and operate as a primary stimulus for growth. Pick targeted accessories with low systemic fatigue ie leg extensions, bicep curls etc. \n\nLast week, week 12, use as a test week with some heavy singles on the main lifts in the rpe 9+ range or 90%+, or deload depending on goals and how you feel.\n\nAfter the program is completed run it again, you should be able to run this for a long time with maybe some minor adjustments to make it suit you.\n\nThis program balances intensity and volume, pushing your limits while allowing for recovery. Everyone is different and if you feel recovery is lagging try my 3 day per week program. Get ready to elevate your performance and achieve the results you've been striving for! No lifetime intermediate lifting life for you! \n\nRecommend doing my 3 days/week first to see how you respond to this type of training.","level":["Intermediate"],"goals":["Powerlifting","Bodybuilding"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":70,"joins":5,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-05T20:11:47.494Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Day 1","Day 2","Day 3","Day 4"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"ss":[{"n":"Reverse Pec Deck","sets":[{"r":15,"rpe":6},{"r":10,"rpe":6}],"note":"Warm-up intensity"},{"n":"Pec Deck (Machine)","sets":[{"r":15,"rpe":6},{"r":10,"rpe":6}],"note":"Warm-up intensity"}]},{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":3,"pct":80},{"r":6,"pct":65,"x":2}]},{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":3,"pct":80},{"r":6,"pct":65,"x":2}]},{"n":"Chest Press (Machine)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":9},{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":10}]},{"n":"Seal Row","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":9},{"lo":8,"hi":1